WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:04.300
In the 1970s, during the Cold War, if you
wanted to see if someone was launching an

00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:08.970
intercontinental ballistic missile at your
country, you had two options: put a satellite

00:00:08.970 --> 00:00:14.660
in space -- which at that point was expensive,
impractical, and likely to fail -- or build

00:00:14.660 --> 00:00:16.179
a radar system.

00:00:16.179 --> 00:00:21.210
But radar can only see to the horizon. It'll
show you a missile in flight, but by the time

00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:25.710
you've seen it your chain of command might
not have time to react. You'd need to know

00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:31.909
it's launched, when it's launched. You need
radar powerful enough to bounce off the ionosphere,

00:00:31.909 --> 00:00:36.450
spread out for thousands of miles, and still
have enough signal to be detectable after

00:00:36.450 --> 00:00:42.950
it bounces back. You need megawatts of power
and one of the biggest radar arrays ever constructed.

00:00:42.950 --> 00:00:46.010
You need something like this.

00:00:46.010 --> 00:00:50.420
Welcome to the Duga-3 Array, in Chernobyl.

00:00:50.420 --> 00:00:54.920
This was known in the west as the Russian
Woodpecker. For more than a decade, it randomly

00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:59.640
hopped shortwave frequencies, trying to find
the best one to get a return signal, sending

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:05.259
out a repetitive rat-t-t-t-t-t-t that sounded
like a woodpecker. It was so powerful that

00:01:05.259 --> 00:01:09.799
countries around the world filed official
complaints with the Soviet Union, and there

00:01:09.799 --> 00:01:14.049
was a small industry of "Woodpecker filters"
or "Moscow mufflers" that would notch it out

00:01:14.049 --> 00:01:14.689
on your radio.

00:01:14.689 --> 00:01:19.039
There's another one about 60km away, they
act as a pair: one transmits, one receives.

00:01:19.039 --> 00:01:24.530
And it needs to be this big to get any sort
of resolution, so you can tell roughly -- very

00:01:24.530 --> 00:01:29.799
roughly -- where the missile's headed. But
the really clever thing is how any radar system

00:01:29.799 --> 00:01:33.859
like this can tell the difference between
a missile and the ground. Because this isn't

00:01:33.859 --> 00:01:38.420
like pointing a radar into the air: if you're
bouncing the signal, then the ground is going

00:01:38.420 --> 00:01:42.829
to reflect back just as much as the missile
that's flying above it. How do you tell the

00:01:42.829 --> 00:01:43.979
difference?

00:01:43.979 --> 00:01:49.049
Doppler effect. The same way that the siren
on a police car moving towards you sounds

00:01:49.049 --> 00:01:53.909
higher, the radar reflections from a missile
will be at a higher frequency. And missiles

00:01:53.909 --> 00:01:59.090
move at speeds measured in miles per second.
So Duga didn't listen for the same frequency

00:01:59.090 --> 00:02:03.549
that its friend transmitted. It listened for
a slightly higher one. If it found that return

00:02:03.549 --> 00:02:09.910
frequency, then -- well. It didn't happen.
Fortunately, all that transmission power,

00:02:09.910 --> 00:02:16.310
all that disruption, was for nothing. Wonderfully.
Because no-one ever launched, and somehow,

00:02:16.310 --> 00:02:19.500
despite everything, humanity got through the
Cold War.

00:02:20.900 --> 00:02:22.900
